\subsection[Phase 4: Convergence Under Large Initial State Uncertainty]{Phase 4: Convergence Under Large Initial State Uncertainty ($\Delta\text{SOC}_0 = -40\%$)}
In real-world BMS operation, initialization errors arise from prolonged parking, self-discharge, or battery replacement. Figure~\ref{fig:conv} and Table~\ref{tab:4_conv} depict the convergence trajectories when an initial error of $\Delta\text{SOC}_0 = -40\%$ ($\hat{z}_0 = 0.50$ versus $\text{SOC}_0^{\text{true}} = 0.90$) is imposed.

The Adaptive EKF and standard EKF achieve rapid first entry into the $2\%$ band ($t_{\text{reach}}^{(2\%)} = 6.0\text{ s}$ and $7.0\text{ s}$) and permanent settling within the $5\%$ operational corridor ($t_{\text{settle}}^{(5\%)} = 6.0\text{ s}$ and $7.0\text{ s}$), maintaining bounded overall RMSE ($0.683\%$ and $0.686\%$) and dynamic peak error $\text{MaxE}_{\text{dyn}} = 4.43\%$ during acceleration pulses. The Sliding Mode Observer first reaches the $2\%$ band in $t_{\text{reach}}^{(2\%)} = 9.0\text{ s}$ ($\text{RMSE} = 1.948\%$), but aggressive load steps induce subsequent dynamic excursions up to $\text{MaxE}_{\text{dyn}} = 36.34\%$ ($t_{\text{settle}}^{(5\%)} = \text{N/A}$). BiLSTM-Attn requires $t_{\text{reach}}^{(2\%)} = 30.0\text{ s}$ to purge erroneous history ($\text{RMSE} = 5.625\%$, $\text{MaxE}_{\text{dyn}} = 39.99\%$, $t_{\text{settle}}^{(5\%)} = \text{N/A}$). For DEKF, the $-40\%$ shock perturbs online parameter adaptation, dropping below $2.5\%$ error by $350\text{ s}$, entering the $\le 2.0\%$ threshold at $t_{\text{reach}}^{(2\%)} = 725.0\text{ s}$, and permanently settling within the $5\%$ corridor at $t_{\text{settle}}^{(5\%)} = 732.0\text{ s}$ ($\text{MaxE}_{\text{dyn}} = 7.67\%$). In contrast, PINN-SOC operates as a feedforward static mapping without recursive settling ($t_{\text{reach}}^{(2\%)} = \text{N/A}$, $t_{\text{settle}}^{(5\%)} = \text{N/A}$, $\text{RMSE} = 2.229\%$, $\text{MaxE}_{\text{dyn}} = 6.84\%$).

