\subsection[Phase 6: Multi-Chemistry Generalization: LiFePO4 (LFP) Flat Plateau]{Phase 6: Multi-Chemistry Generalization: $\text{LiFePO}_4$ (LFP) Flat Plateau}
Figure~\ref{fig:lfp} and Table~\ref{tab:5_lfp} report estimation performance on LFP chemistry. In the plateau region ($20\%\text{--}80\%$ SOC), the slope $d V_{\text{ocv}} / d\,\text{SOC}$ falls near zero ($\approx 0.08\text{ V}$), causing the observability Gramian condition number $\kappa(\mathcal{O})$ to surge. 

Consequently, $\mathbf{H}_k \approx [0, -1, -1, -1]$ loses SOC sensitivity. Standard EKF degrades by $+1648.4\%$, escalating from $0.065\%$ on NMC to $1.136\%$ on LFP ($\text{MAE} = 0.939\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 2.410\%$), while AEKF degrades by $+281.1\%$ to $2.893\%$ RMSE and SMO chatters along the plateau ($+919.5\%$ to $9.593\%$ RMSE, $\text{MaxE} = 20.935\%$). 

In contrast, DEKF achieves the lowest error among all observers on LFP ($\text{RMSE} = 0.870\%$ [$\text{A123-3}$: $0.861\%$, $\text{A123-4}$: $0.879\%$], $\text{MAE} = 0.649\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 2.018\%$). Crucially, this tracking is primarily driven by accurate open-loop Coulomb integration supported by continuous capacity tracking ($\hat{Q}_{\text{act}}$) rather than closed-loop voltage correction: while online adaptation of $R_0$ and $Q_{\text{act}}$ does not restore direct voltage-to-SOC observability within the flat plateau ($V_{\text{ocv}}' \approx 0.08\text{ V}$), it continuously calibrates the Coulomb integration gain $\eta\Delta t/(3600\hat{Q}_{\text{act}})$ and removes ohmic bias $I_k\Delta R_0$, bounding open-loop drift across the plateau without auxiliary strain sensors~\cite{Tian2025}.

