\subsection{Phase 7: Sensor Corruption and Environmental Stress Testing}
Table~\ref{tab:6_sensor} and Fig.~\ref{fig:sensor} document observer resilience under a $+0.25\text{ A}$ current sensor Hall bias drift and $25\text{ mV}$ high-frequency inverter EMI noise ripple.

Open-loop Coulomb counting diverges linearly, accumulating $12.399\%$ end-of-cycle drift ($\text{RMSE} = 7.178\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 12.409\%$). Closed-loop Kalman filters arrest this divergence: EKF limits RMSE to $1.817\%$ ($1.821\%$ drift, $\text{MaxE} = 5.013\%$), while AEKF and DEKF bound RMSE to $1.859\%$ and $2.121\%$ ($2.026\%$ and $1.927\%$ drift). SMO achieves the lowest end-of-cycle drift ($1.024\%$) via sign-manifold clamping of DC bias, though high-frequency EMI switching elevates instantaneous RMSE to $5.035\%$ ($\text{MaxE} = 14.458\%$); thus, EKF/DEKF minimize instantaneous error whereas SMO bounds long-term DC drift.

