\subsection{Phase 8: Model-Based Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS) Analysis Across Aging}
Figure~\ref{fig:eis} depicts model-based Randles--CPE Nyquist spectra ($100\text{ kHz}\text{--}1\text{ mHz}$) simulated via $Z(j\omega) = R_0 + (R_{\text{ct}} + Z_w) / [1 + (j\omega)^\alpha Q_{\text{cpe}} (R_{\text{ct}} + Z_w)]$ with $Z_w(j\omega) = \sigma_w \omega^{-1/2}(1 - j)$, parameterized from Oxford EIS aging ratios~\cite{Birkl2017diagnostics} ($Q_{\text{cpe}} = 1.2\text{ F}\cdot\text{s}^{\alpha-1}$, $\alpha = 0.88$, $\sigma_w = 0.045\ \Omega\cdot\text{s}^{-1/2}$, RMS fitting error $1.1\%\text{--}1.8\%$ across $100\%\text{--}80\%$ SOH). High-frequency SEI resistance $R_{\text{sei}}$ is lumped into effective series resistance $R_0(\text{SOH}) = R_{\text{ohm}} + R_{\text{sei}}$ ($35.0\text{--}52.5\text{ m}\Omega$, $+50\%$), while charge-transfer resistance $R_{\text{ct}}$ widens by $+150\%$ ($15.0\text{--}37.5\text{ m}\Omega$). In the 2-RC transfer function $Z_{\text{2RC}}(s) = R_0 + \sum_{i=1}^2 R_i/(1 + s\tau_i)$ ($\tau_i = R_i C_i$), aging induces an impedance mismatch $\Delta Z(s) = \Delta R_0 + \sum_{i=1}^2 [R_i(\text{SOH})/(1 + s\tau_i(\text{SOH})) - R_{i,\text{nom}}/(1 + s\tau_{i,\text{nom}})]$, generating polarization residual $\Delta V_{\text{pol}}(t) = \mathcal{L}^{-1}\{\Delta Z(s) I(s)\}$ ($\approx I \cdot \Delta R_1$ for $t \gg \tau_1$). Fixed-parameter EKF misattributes $\Delta V_{\text{pol}}(t)$ to $V_{\text{ocv}}(z)$, driving the 45.7-fold RMSE surge; standard DEKF adapts $R_0$ online but omits $R_1$ to avoid collinearity, leaving $\Delta V_{\text{pol}}(t)$ to be compensated by PIDKF.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{../results/fig8_eis_nyquist_aging.png}
\caption{Model-based EIS Nyquist spectra across progressive aging stages ($100\%$ down to $80\%$ SOH)\label{fig:eis}}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Phase 9: Module and Pack-Level Scaling (8S Series Pack)}
Table~\ref{tab:7_pack} and Fig.~\ref{fig:pack} evaluate algorithmic scalability to an 8S series battery pack under realistic manufacturing tolerances (capacity dispersion $\sigma_Q = 3.0\%$, resistance dispersion $\sigma_R = 10.0\%$)~\cite{Yang2024degradation,Zheng2018inconsistency}. In automotive strings, boundary thermal dissipation gradients cause core cells (cells~\#4 and~\#5) to operate at $+3.2^\circ\text{C}$ above perimeter cells~\#1 and~\#8. Via Arrhenius kinetics (Eqs.~(9)--(10)), this thermal asymmetry accelerates SEI growth and lithium loss on cell~\#4, causing its capacity to fade $1.38\times$ faster than the string mean.

Under dynamic UDDS driving, a lumped observer treats the string as a homogeneous cell driven by mean voltage $V_{\text{string}}/8$. While tracking pack-average state ($\text{RMSE} = 0.128\%$, $\text{MaxE} = 0.443\%$), it is blind to cell divergence: as the weakest cell (cell~\#4) nears cutoff ($2.50\text{ V}$), the lumped model incurs \textbf{1.744\% RMSE and 2.302\% peak error}, risking severe copper dissolution and lithium plating.

In contrast, distributed DEKF tracks individual cell states while recursively identifying each cell's ohmic and polarization parameters online, confining weakest-cell RMSE to $0.337\%$ and peak error to $0.963\%$ for deterministic active-balancing margins without per-cell EIS hardware.

