\subsection{Phase 10: Embedded Hardware Execution on ARM Cortex-M and PIDKF Superiority}\label{sec:hw_profiling}
Computational complexity was measured via hardware \texttt{DWT->CYCCNT} cycle counters on ARM Cortex-M4 (STM32F407VG, 168~MHz) and projected via constant-IPC clock-scaling to Cortex-M7 (480~MHz) using CMSIS-DSP (Table~\ref{tab:8_embedded}, Fig.~\ref{fig:embedded}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{../results/fig10_embedded_resource_tradeoff.png}
\caption{Pareto efficiency frontier: SOC estimation RMSE versus measured ARM Cortex-M4 execution latency on degraded cells ($85\%$ SOH, UDDS), contrasted against fresh-cell baselines ($100\%$ SOH)\label{fig:embedded}}
\end{figure}

 SMO demonstrates ultra-light overhead (180~FLOPs, 140~B RAM, $1.67\;\mu\text{s}$ measured on M4, $0.58\;\mu\text{s}$ projected on M7), while DEKF requires 980~FLOPs, 620~B SRAM, and $8.63\;\mu\text{s}$ on Cortex-M4. Conversely, BiLSTM-Attn demands $245,000$ FLOPs and $18.4\text{ KB}$ SRAM ($2440.48\;\mu\text{s}$ per cell), translating to $39.04\text{ ms}$ across 16 series cells ($39.0\%$ CPU load in a $100\text{ ms}$ loop), impractical for multicell MCUs.

Table~\ref{tab:9_pidkf} and Fig.~\ref{fig:pidkf} summarize the \textbf{PIDKF} ablation study on degraded cells ($85\%$ SOH, 5 seeds, test cells \texttt{INR20R-3,4} and \texttt{A123-3,4}), revealing chemistry-dependent mechanisms. On $\text{LiFePO}_4$ plateaus, dynamic hysteresis tracking dominates, cutting RMSE from $0.302\%$ to $0.149\% \pm 0.008\%$ ($\text{A123-3}$: $0.145\%$, $\text{A123-4}$: $0.153\%$; $\text{SD}_{\text{cell}} = \pm 0.006\%$; $0.335\%$ MaxE) as two-phase coexistence governs hysteresis; neural compensation alone yields $0.246\% \pm 0.011\%$ RMSE. Conversely, on NMC cells, the physics-informed neural compensator dominates, reducing peak error by $46.6\%$ ($1.400\%$ to $0.748\% \pm 0.024\%$) and RMSE to $0.414\% \pm 0.012\%$ ($\text{INR20R-3}$: $0.408\%$, $\text{INR20R-4}$: $0.420\%$; $\text{SD}_{\text{cell}} = \pm 0.009\%$) during high-rate pulses, whereas unconstrained black-box MLP degrades accuracy ($0.438\% \pm 0.018\%$ NMC, $0.285\% \pm 0.014\%$ LFP) via overfitting. Comparing Exact-Jacobian PIDKF ($\mathbf{H}_{\text{PIDKF},k}$, $3,806$ cycles, $22.65\;\mu\text{s}$) against Exogenous-Jacobian PIDKF ($\mathbf{H}_k$, $2,386$ cycles, $14.20\;\mu\text{s}$) in Table~\ref{tab:9_pidkf} confirms near-identical accuracy ($0.412\%$ vs.\ $0.414\%$ NMC; $0.148\%$ vs.\ $0.149\%$ LFP) while cutting MCU execution by $37.3\%$.

Beyond the $85\%$ SOH ablation, PIDKF was benchmarked across the complete degradation trajectory ($100\%, 90\%, 85\%, 80\%$ SOH) and thermal operating window ($0^\circ\text{C}, 25^\circ\text{C}, 45^\circ\text{C}$). Across aging stages at $25^\circ\text{C}$, PIDKF consistently maintains sub-$0.5\%$ RMSE: for NMC, RMSE is $0.185\%$ ($100\%$), $0.326\%$ ($90\%$), $0.414\%$ ($85\%$), and $0.482\%$ ($80\%$ SOH, $\text{ASF} = +160.5\%$ vs.\ $+4473.8\%$ for EKF); for LFP, RMSE remains tightly bounded at $0.112\%$ ($100\%$), $0.134\%$ ($90\%$), $0.149\%$ ($85\%$), and $0.178\%$ ($80\%$ SOH). Under thermal stress at $85\%$ SOH, PIDKF restricts cold-temperature ($0^\circ\text{C}$) error to $0.842\%$ (NMC) and $0.386\%$ (LFP), while elevated-temperature ($45^\circ\text{C}$) errors are $0.435\%$ (NMC) and $0.162\%$ (LFP). Crucially, under large initial error ($\Delta\text{SOC}_0 = -40\%$), PIDKF achieves rapid first entry $t_{\text{reach}}^{(2\%)} = 18.0\text{ s}$ and permanent settling $t_{\text{settle}}^{(5\%)} = 22.0\text{ s}$ ($\text{RMSE} = 0.512\%$, $\text{MaxE}_{\text{dyn}} = 3.82\%$), dramatically outperforming standard DEKF ($725.0\text{ s}$) because neural compensation prevents transient voltage shocks from destabilizing parameter identification. Under $+0.25\text{ A}$ Hall current bias, PIDKF limits RMSE to $1.428\%$ and end-of-cycle drift to $0.842\%$, confirming comprehensive robustness across operational phases.









